R30 REHEARSAL — PASS (container path NOT EXERCISED: no Docker daemon)

── what each gate means ───────────────────────────────────────────────
The container path is ENVIRONMENTAL on this host: there is no Docker daemon, so
`docker compose` cannot be driven and the image rollback in
rollback-runbook.md is procedure, not evidence. What CAN be exercised is the
artifact the image carries. Swapping `.next/standalone` + `.next/static` is the
standalone analogue of rolling an image digest back: the same claim that the
bytes which served before serve again, minus the daemon.

  previous artifact   6teqv2zm5azzpKU0nakv3, built 20:06 from the tree at 7c3c2a2
  new artifact        Q0Vyn2Fqoa0Yn_j5UK-H-, built by this run at HEAD bfed32f

Both artifacts booted, both answered liveness, and both completed a real
compress-pdf job on a 300-page fixture and returned a PDF the client could read.
The rollback was not a config toggle: the HEAD build was removed from the tree
and the kept directory copied back in its place before the boot.

Readiness answered 503 `toolchain: false` on both, which is the truthful answer
on a host where `soffice` genuinely is absent (readiness-r23.log). A 503 was
accepted by this gate only because the response names which subsystem is false —
a 503 that said nothing would have failed it.

The two downloads differ by four bytes (149102 vs 149106) for the same input.
That is Ghostscript writing a different creation timestamp and document id into
each output, not a corrupted result: both start with %PDF- and both were fetched
over HTTP 200 through the signed-URL redirect.

The database leg is deliberately NOT repeated here. The online `backup()`
snapshot and its byte-exact restore, including `prisma migrate status` against
the restored file, are already proven in migration-restore-drill.log (R22/§15).
Repeating it would add a second copy of the same evidence, not a second fact.

── the first run of this script FAILED, and the failure was its own ───
FAIL  a real job completes on the new artifact and returns a PDF — download was not a PDF

The database showed the job `completed` at the same second. The script had sent
the POST and then polled the GET with no cookie jar, so the submitter and the
poller were two different anonymous callers, and `/api/jobs/<id>` answered:

  $ curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/jobs/<id>            # no session
  {"error":"Job not found."}
  $ curl -sS -b jar http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/jobs/<id>     # the submitter
  {"id":"cmtm32vhn000buob4xpdgazy1","status":"running","progress":null,
   "result":null,"error":null,"errorType":null}

That 404 is the R12 ownership rule working exactly as designed — a stranger gets
404 rather than 403, and never a stranger's bytes. Reported as a product failure
it would have been a deployment blocker that does not exist. Classified PROBE
DEFECT and fixed in the script (`bfed32f`): one cookie jar per call, carried on
every hop that belongs to the submitter.

The same run surfaced a second thing about the download: it answers 302 to a
signed URL on the public origin, whose certificate is self-signed here, so the
fetch needs -L -k. Cookies are NOT carried to that hop — the jar is scoped to
127.0.0.1 and the redirect goes to 172.20.10.2, so curl drops them itself, which
is the right test because the signed URL is supposed to authorize itself.

── not exercised ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
No Docker build, no `docker compose up`, no image digest pinned, no
`prisma migrate deploy` inside a container, no load balancer draining on the 503.
Zero-downtime deployment was not attempted: this rehearsal stops the server,
swaps the artifact and starts it again, which is a restart with an outage window
of a few seconds, and that is what the runbook describes.
